course: dictionary
lesson: 13323
title: Grubby: dirty or not quite honest
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives grubby two meanings: rather dirty, and not quite honest. The words around it tell you which one the writer means. «Ben came in from the garden with grubby hands.» Here the garden tells you the hands are dirty from mud and soil.
explain: «The shopkeeper's grubby little trick cheated the customers.» Trick and cheated are the clues. A trick that cheats people is not honest, so grubby means not quite honest here. Always ask: what does the sentence say about this word? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it makes sense.
explain: Grubby is often used with hands, knees, fingers and clothes. Adults say, «Wash those grubby hands before dinner», because dirt can carry germs. When you meet a word with two meanings, pick the one that fits the rest of the sentence best.
fact: Grubby means rather dirty, usually from mud, sweat or touching things.
fact: Grubby can also mean not quite honest or decent, as in «a grubby little trick».
fact: Grubby hands, grubby knees and grubby clothes are common phrases.
fact: Washing grubby hands removes dirt and germs, so it helps keep us healthy.
fact: Grubby is an adjective, and it changes to grubbier and grubbiest when comparing.
exercise: «Ben came in from the garden with grubby hands.» What does grubby mean here? | *Rather dirty from mud and soil | Loud and noisy | Very clean and shiny | Cold and wet from the rain
exercise: «The shopkeeper's grubby little trick cheated the customers.» What does grubby mean here? | Covered in mud | Brightly coloured | Very large | *Not quite honest
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to grubby, meaning rather dirty? | quiet | dry | bright | *dirty
test: «The old dog was grubby after rolling in the mud.» What made the dog grubby? | It was fast asleep | It had a brand new collar | It had just been washed | *It had mud all over it
test: In which phrase does grubby mean not quite honest? | grubby fingers | grubby clothes | *a grubby little trick | grubby knees
exam: «The children's grubby clothes went straight into the wash.» What does grubby mean here? | Wet from the rain | *Dirty from play | Brand new | Torn into holes
